WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.930
Today, I'm gonna stray a bit from
the subject of language learning.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:09.210
Uh, the other day I made a video about
the importance of being curious and the

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:13.800
importance of continuing to learn and,
and, and, uh, not only to challenge

00:00:13.805 --> 00:00:15.600
our race, but to learn new things.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.700
Uh, and the evidence that that's very
positive for us as we grow older, and

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.140
really very positive at any age, to be
curious and to be constantly learning.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:30.330
And, uh, a number of comments were
very positive towards me and, you

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:35.099
know, thanking me for the, what they're
considered to be an encouraging message.

00:00:35.099 --> 00:00:40.230
And people who are at, you know, either
50 or 60 or 70 were saying, yes, thank

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:44.519
you, and I'm the same way, and I, I'm
learning and then that's keeping me young.

00:00:44.519 --> 00:00:46.680
And, and I, I think all
of that is, is positive.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:47.551
And I know a lot of people...

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.629
I mean, we have only to look
at the two likely presidential

00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:53.069
candidates in the United States.

00:00:53.069 --> 00:00:59.880
One is 77, the other is 80, uh, to
realize that older people are still very

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.210
active, uh, and with a lot to contribute.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:10.680
But I also wanted to say that as a person
who is obviously in the older category,

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:19.650
that I think older people in a way may
not always realize the extent to which

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.020
we are indebted to younger people.

00:01:23.475 --> 00:01:29.295
It particularly came to light during
the Covid pandemic when younger people

00:01:29.295 --> 00:01:31.304
had to sacrifice for the older people.

00:01:32.205 --> 00:01:37.065
Younger people, who tend to be more
active out, more restaurants or concerts

00:01:37.065 --> 00:01:42.054
or wherever, and they were forced to
forego some of these activities or

00:01:43.545 --> 00:01:49.335
other restrictions which were imposed
on all of society, even though it was

00:01:49.335 --> 00:01:52.815
really largely the vulnerable, not
exclusively, like there were a lot

00:01:52.815 --> 00:01:56.005
of younger people who also got very
ill and ended up in a hospital in the

00:01:56.335 --> 00:02:01.395
ICU and so forth, but overwhelmingly
was older people who suffered and

00:02:01.395 --> 00:02:04.935
yet all of society and in particular
younger people had to sacrifice.

00:02:05.245 --> 00:02:09.824
And when you then look at how many
societies are structured today, I

00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:13.895
can only speak for Vancouver, but
the cost of housing is out of sight.

00:02:14.415 --> 00:02:16.425
Younger people can't afford to buy homes.

00:02:16.905 --> 00:02:22.425
Uh, older people, I think statistically
have more wealth than younger people,

00:02:22.875 --> 00:02:27.885
and yet in our society, seniors
get a discount on public transit.

00:02:28.365 --> 00:02:34.035
Uh, the health system overwhelmingly
looks after older people.

00:02:34.875 --> 00:02:37.095
And the younger people
of course, pay taxes.

00:02:37.095 --> 00:02:39.075
They work and they
support the older people.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:45.510
So older people and, and of course over
we, it's older people who vote more than

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:46.920
younger people, which is kind of silly.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.660
Younger people should get
out there and vote more.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:54.330
But, uh, you know, rather than me paying
a, you know, having a discount on the

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:57.090
buses here, in a way I should pay more.

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:03.570
Um, because I, people of my generation
are a bigger burden on the health system.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.600
Maybe we should pay more
into the health system.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:10.710
Now, obviously you have people
who are not well off, uh, in old

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:14.820
age, and those people should get,
you know, assistance of some kind.

00:03:15.269 --> 00:03:18.810
But a lot of people my
age are doing just fine.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:24.390
And so in a way they should be
contributing more back into society.

00:03:25.575 --> 00:03:28.005
Uh, you know, even something
like global warming.

00:03:28.155 --> 00:03:32.445
Overwhelmingly, scientists believe that we
are going to have increasing difficulties.

00:03:32.565 --> 00:03:37.635
I know this is controversial to some,
I don't, I'm not a scientist, but the

00:03:37.635 --> 00:03:42.555
vast majority of scientists believe
that there will be predominantly

00:03:42.734 --> 00:03:48.644
negative economic impact from climate
change, and the older people are not

00:03:48.644 --> 00:03:52.635
gonna be facing these difficulties,
whereas the younger generation are.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:56.640
And so for any of these different
points of view, it's fine to

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.150
respect older people, thank you
very much for having, you know,

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.520
live their lives and, and so forth.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:06.330
And traditionally we respect people
with white or gray hair, which is fine.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:10.650
Uh, and I think older people
tend to be perhaps physically

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.421
weaker or frailer or can be.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.170
So I think they should
be treated with respect.

00:04:16.175 --> 00:04:23.640
However, I think there should be some
recognition that older people maybe

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:28.680
should be in the tax system or in
the extent to which older people have

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.010
a free ride in the health system,
at least in countries like Canada

00:04:32.015 --> 00:04:36.970
where we have a basically a universal
coverage system, that older people...

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:43.140
I, I wouldn't mind paying, uh, you know,
uh, I wouldn't call it wealthy seniors

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:48.914
tax, but something that recognizes
that, um, you know, I am taking out

00:04:48.914 --> 00:04:53.025
more or potentially if I were to
get ill, Well I presumably will at

00:04:53.025 --> 00:04:55.544
some point as I continue along here.

00:04:55.995 --> 00:04:59.745
Um, we should be contributing
more and giving the younger

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:01.005
generation a better break.

00:05:01.425 --> 00:05:04.395
So just some vague thoughts on that.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.705
Uh, I'm sure that'll stir up a hornets
nest of comment and I can't say that

00:05:09.705 --> 00:05:13.905
I've thought these things through
carefully, nor have I really researched

00:05:14.025 --> 00:05:18.075
this, but it's just a feeling that
I had after seeing a lot of the

00:05:18.075 --> 00:05:21.795
positive comments about me talking
about aging and learning and so forth.

00:05:21.795 --> 00:05:28.005
So I think seniors also should be
contributing more to society than, than

00:05:28.005 --> 00:05:29.625
they already have through their lives.

00:05:29.805 --> 00:05:34.875
But even now at, at this stage, uh, you
know, at, at my stage in life, uh, I

00:05:34.965 --> 00:05:36.645
believe we should be contributing more.

00:05:37.155 --> 00:05:38.355
So there you have it.

